body: |-
  THE GAP (from v1): admit() DECIDED every concurrent merge (FAST_ADMIT/CLEAN/KNOT/DANGLE, proven where git conflicts) but on a CLEAN re-base did not PRODUCE the merged tree. v2 closes it.

  #merge3 (the cardinal-sin component — a wrong merge silently corrupts): tokenize splits text into word/whitespace/punct runs (join===text). merge3 is a hunk-based diff3 over token arrays: LCS alignment → change hunks (base ranges + replacements) → walk merging by base position. A range changed by only ONE side takes that side; disjoint ranges compose; ranges BOTH changed in OVERLAPPING base coordinates are CONFLICTS (counted, never silently resolved). The first cut used a 'stable-in-both-anchors' approach that FALSELY conflicted a deletion next to an edit (lumped them in one inter-anchor gap); the test caught it and the hunk-based-with-overlap-detection rewrite fixed it. Token granularity is the key: git merges whole LINES (so two edits to one line conflict); token merge sees the edits touch different tokens.

  #materialize: computeMerge(base, ours, theirs refs) reads each changed file via git and merges per-file; add/delete-vs-edit or any overlap → surfaced conflict. materializeMergedState applies the conflict-free plan onto a throwaway copy of the base tree (git archive) and absorbs it → the merged WarpState; the user's working tree is never touched. BYTE-FIDELITY BUG caught by the test: the shared git() runner trims stdout, so gitShow dropped trailing newlines → byte drift. Fixed gitShow to be byte-faithful (no trim). The stateId matched anyway (essences ignore whitespace) but byte-exactness is non-negotiable for a VCS.

  #admit v2: on FAST_ADMIT seal the proposed state; on CLEAN call materializeMergedState (base/theirs git commits come from the strands' provenance.gitCommit anchors; ours = the agent's ref), seal the merged strand, advance selvage, clear scratch. If materialization finds a byte conflict the meaning layer missed → downgrade to KNOT (never a silent wrong-merge). Needs git refs (coexistence byte store); a WORKTREE proposal returns CLEAN unsealed.

  PROOF: materialize.test — A edits foo, B edits bar on ONE line → git merge-tree CONFLICTS, computeMerge produces the byte-exact merged file, materializeMergedState's stateId equals the hand-authored both-edits branch. GUARD — both edit foo's return token → conflict, state null. admit-seal.test (end-to-end) — genesis(base) → fork B at genesis → A admits (FAST, seals A) → B admits (CLEAN re-base, MATERIALIZED + sealed) → selvage == both-edits state, fabric depth 3.

  REMAINING (T-2026-06-26-002, Phase C v3): per-domain optimistic CAS (admission is currently serialized via the single selvage pointer; disjoint-domain WEFTs should admit concurrently without a global lock); KNOT council UX (structured resolution + paradigm_decision_record); grade calibratedConfidence via the classroom outcome loop.

  Commit b8be7999. The multi-writer path of a true git replacement now works: Warpline performs merges git can't.</parameter>
